  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure has led to a dry but cool day across SE Michigan. Many \nlocations have seen temperature readings hold in the upper 60s, even \nunder plenty of sunshine. Anticyclonic surface wind pattern over \nwestern Michigan identifies the center of the surface high, which \nwill drift overhead this evening and cause winds to back to the \nsoutheast. Flow backs further to the southwest overnight, drawing a \ntheta-e ridge into the Great Lakes region Tuesday. This brings \nwarmer temperatures, increased rain chances, and breezy southwest \nflow to SE Michigan. \n\nThe theta-e surge will be aided initially by a belt of ~50 to 60 \nknot h8 winds late tonight, driven on the larger scale by SE CONUS \nhigh pressure. The nose of the jet reaches northern Michigan early \nTuesday morning, focusing a broad area of precipitation toward the \nSaginaw Valley for the first half of the day. A deep closed low \nacross Ontario will simultaneously drag a cold front across northern \nCONUS, which will push rain chances eastward and thus into the rest \nof the CWA through the day Tuesday. One questionable aspect of this \nsystem is how well the stratiform rain can hold up against the dry \nairmass sampled overhead today (PWAT of 0.44\" per 12z KDTX sounding \nfor reference). Soundings are showing a good amount of dry air \nsurviving at the low levels, which could eat into QPF amounts and \neven rain chances altogether, especially as the low level jet \nforcing departs mid-day Tuesday. The waning LLJ presence will still \nbe enough to produce breezy conditions with gusts of 25 to 30 mph \npossible. \n\nAs the cold front and associated moisture convergence arrive Tuesday \nafternoon-evening, event character becomes more convective with \nshowers and thunderstorms redeveloping along the boundary. \nInstability is not expected to be overly impressive, but several \nmodels bring 70 degree dewpoints across the state line Tuesday \nevening which is enough for about 1000 J/kg MLCAPE. This occurs in a \nfairly tight time window between about 21z-01z (5pm-9pm) south of I-\n94. SWODY2 continues to designate a Marginal Risk for severe weather \nacross Lenawee/Monroe Counties, mainly for isolated precipitation \nloaded downbursts in a marginal shear environment. The broader \nthreat looks to be heavy rainfall, especially given the high \nmoisture environment and proximity of the frontal zone. More details \nare in the Hydrology section. \n\nSE Michigan through the rest of the work week will be in the hands \nof the frontal zone. The baroclinic zone certainly stays within \nreach of us, but models generally shove it into the Ohio Valley for \nWednesday before lifting back north Thursday-Friday. Broad brushed \nPoPs reflect the uncertainty in coverage/timing of this pattern \nrather than a full wash out over the next several days. Wavering \nnature of the frontal zone will also cause temperatures to waffle \nbetween 70s and 80s through the rest of the work week with unsettled \nconditions possibly extending into the weekend.",
